  "summary": "An external structural support that counteracts the lateral thrust of vaults or arches.",
  "description": "Characterized by its arched form extending from the exterior wall to a pier or foundation, allowing for taller, thinner walls and larger windows. This Gothic innovation enabled the construction of vast cathedrals with unprecedented height and light, revolutionizing ecclesiastical architecture.",
  "culturalContext": "The buttress — external masonry bracing that receives the sideways thrust of vaults and roofs — made Gothic possible: from the simple angled pilaster buttress of Romanesque churches to the flying buttress (arcus volans) that Abbot Suger's choir and Notre-Dame de Paris turned into structural lacework. Beyond engineering, buttressing became iconography — cathedral silhouettes are buttress silhouettes — and its structural honesty was claimed by Gothic Revivalists and Viollet-le-Duc's rationalist theory as the essence of true building.",
